! Hirshfeld atom refinement of a covalent network solid -- quartz -- using
! nearest-neighbour atom-centred fragments at level 1 with hydrogen capping.
! RHF/def2-SVP. This is the L1+H model of
!
!    M. L. Davidson, "Beyond X-rays: a critical analysis of quantum chemistry
!    based X-ray structure refinement and wavefunction fitting techniques",
!    PhD thesis, UWA, June 2023, chapter 5,
!
! which reported the first HAR of a covalent network solid. Two fragments,
! one per asymmetric unit atom: orthosilicic acid around the silicon and
! silyloxysilane around the oxygen.
!
! The job refines TWICE, and both sets of results are in this output. The
! first refinement is the L1+H model as published, with no extinction
! correction, and it is the one the agreement table below refers to. The
! second switches the SHELXL extinction correction on and refines again from
! the result of the first. About 40 s for the pair.
!
! The switch is the top-level optimise_extinction= keyword, which reaches
! .crystal.xray_data directly. Nothing is reallocated and no earlier input is
! repeated.
!
! Quartz is the extinction test case: hard, well ordered, and the correction
! is unmistakable. Measured 2026-08-23:
!
!                        no extinction   with extinction
!    N_p                 19              20
!    GoF^2               9.8368          8.3878
!    R(F)                0.0120          0.0105
!    Rw(F)               0.0130          0.0120
!    scale               1.0025          1.0101
!    extinction          --              1.8093(1298)
!
! The extinction parameter is fourteen times its own error, the GoF^2 falls by
! 15% and R(F) by 12%. N_p rises by exactly one, which is the point of the
! parameter-count work in docs/EXTINCTION_REPORT.md section 4. Compare urea,
! tests/hart/urea_hart_STO-3G, where the same correction finds nothing: 1.3
! sigma on a small crystal, as the consensus says it should.
!
! AGREEMENT WITH THE THESIS, measured 2026-08-15. The thesis HF/def2-SVP L1
! column against this job:
!
!                        this job      thesis
!    R(F) / R1           0.0120        0.0127
!    GoF^2               9.8368        10.610
!    r(Si-O) /A          1.609(8)      1.610(6)
!    U_iso(Si) /A^2      0.0029(7)     0.0029(5)
!    U_iso(O)  /A^2      0.00566(4)    0.00567(4)
!
! The structural parameters agree to the digits printed. R and GoF^2 sit 5-7%
! apart, which is what a different data reduction should give: this job has
! 1009 reflections, the thesis had 1134. See the note in quartz_init.cif.
!
! Two results worth keeping in mind when this reference is next re-blessed:
!
!  * HAR does NOT beat the independent atom model here. The thesis IAM gives
!    GoF^2 7.235 against this L1+H HAR's 9.84. That is the thesis's central
!    negative finding and it reproduces.
!
!  * The reported bond length su, 0.008 A, is roughly 50x the SHELX IAM su on
!    the SAME data (1.60925(15) A). The inflation tracks the SILICON, which
!    sits on a 2-fold axis: refined position sus are 0.009 A for Si against
!    0.00014 A for the general-position oxygen. Tonto refines all coordinates
!    and projects onto the invariant subspace afterwards, rather than refining
!    only the independent ones, so special-position sus come out of a
!    rank-deficient pseudo-inverse. The bond length VALUE is good to 0.0003 A;
!    it is only the uncertainty that is wrong.
!
! Known output defects, present in this reference and NOT to be blessed away:
! Rw(F2) prints NaN, R_sigma(F2) prints 0.0000, and every reflection is
! reported as an unmatched Friedel pair.

   input:   quartz_init.cif
   input:   HKLdata.quartz
